design:
Baseline: profile_plan.rs owns ConsumerProfileStep, PlannedStep, ProfilePlanDetailCode and profile_plan_config_error; the architecture paragraph naming scheduler.rs as error-code owner is stale and is corrected by deliverable 5. scheduler.rs retains scheduling only. test_selectors.rs:627/658 parse env/cwd. Extract the validation into private step_exec.rs; retain the test-selector error strings through its caller adapter.
```rust
struct StepExecError { field: String, cause: String }
fn parse_env(value: Option<&toml::Value>) -> Result<BTreeMap<String, String>, StepExecError>;
fn parse_cwd(value: Option<&toml::Value>) -> Result<Option<PathBuf>, StepExecError>;
// ConsumerProfileStep / PlannedStep: env: BTreeMap<String,String>, cwd: Option<PathBuf>
// StepPlan keeps env: Vec<(OsString,OsString)>, cwd: Option<PathBuf>; conversion is lossless.
// ProfilePlanDetailCode adds unit variants InvalidStepEnv and InvalidStepCwd.
fn step_log_file_name(expanded_plan_index: usize, name: &str) -> String;
```
Profile detail strings: PROFILE.INVALID_STEP_ENV / PROFILE.INVALID_STEP_CWD; top-level CLI.SC_LINT_PROFILE_CONFIG_INVALID. Preserve step name and field env.<KEY> / cwd; recovery fixes that field; docs reference sc-lint docs configuration. Test-selector adapters preserve their current error envelope/text; no duplicated parser.
step_logs.rs is the single step-output I/O owner. step_log_file_name uses the stable zero-based expanded-plan ordinal formatted with at least four digits, a hyphen, and the step name with every byte outside ASCII letters/digits/_/- replaced by underscore; an empty safe name becomes step. Thus slash, backslash and every dot (including '..') are removed, and distinct expanded steps, including adapted variants, cannot collide. File::create truncates that step's file on each run. Create only the steps/ subdirectory under the already-resolved logger root; leave JSONL logs and parent environment unchanged. Write controlled raw stdout/stderr bytes in labelled [stdout] and [stderr] sections, preserving each stream's content; stdout and stderr are both visible on failure. Successful steps also get files. Report create/write failures through the existing structured I/O error path with destination and cause. No new scheduler or logger-root resolution is added.
